{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== HANDOFF: e369 CLOSED (force, documented). What remains is a GATE-DEFECT CLUSTER. ===\n\n--- WHERE THINGS STAND ---\n**Epic e369 (Jira Server/DC support) is CLOSED**, 2026-08-05, via `--force-close` on explicit operator\ndirection, after a manual evidenced verification of all 14 acceptance criteria against `gerrit/main`\n(see the \"MANUAL AC VERIFICATION\" comment on the epic — each criterion checked with a file:line or a\nrun id, not a checkbox). `signature: None` plus a `FORCE_CLOSE` audit record are present: this close\nrecords **operator authorization + manual verification, NOT a passing completion verification.** Do\nnot read it as an attestation.\n\nDelivered and verified: **59/59 descendants closed, 0 uncertified** (each carrying a real\n`completion-verifier` signature; each change merged through `Verified +1` AND `LLM-Review +1`), and\nthe exit bar green on `main` — harness run **30986591446** at `0df44dcbc9`: 62 passed, 1 xfailed,\n**0 failed, 0 errored**, canary `collected=63 executed=62 skipped=1`.\n\nClosing e369 UNBLOCKED **5796** (DC hardening follow-on) and **708d** (Markdown<->Jira rich text).\n\n--- WHY THE FORCE WAS NECESSARY (do not \"just retry\" it) ---\nThe completion verifier **cannot produce a verdict for this epic**. Four exhaustions: 480, 1600,\n480-with-the-`search_files`-fix, and an instrumented reproduction. [rebar:c827-2a23-cd00-473e] proved\nit is NOT capacity:\n* Three of four instrumented runs **LOOPED**. SPLIT-A repeated one identical `search_files` call\n  **144 times**; SPLIT-B looped 62x on an *honest* `(no matches)`. Repetition wasted 40-70% of every\n  pathological run.\n* The one clean run (238 distinct / 258 calls, ratio 0.922) exhausts on **genuine breadth** against a\n  flat 240-request budget — which, per operator intent (the budget bounds FAILURES, not capacity), is\n  correct behaviour rather than a bug to route around.\n* A resource exhaustion is **not a verdict**, and the gate's only documented exit from that state is\n  `--force-close` — the trap that steers agents into the unattested state the project forbids.\n\n--- THE BLOCKING CHAIN (start here) ---\n    c827  wire loop detection to ACT (it is currently report-only)\n     |-- 70bc (P1) BLOCKER: `max_consecutive_repeat` is INVERTED — counts only ADJACENT repeats, so a\n     |            real 4-call cycle scores 1 while the HEALTHY run scores 5. Wiring an actuator to\n     |            today's metric fires on healthy runs and misses real cycles. Replacement proven:\n     |            windowed distinct-ratio (field `distinct_ratio_window`, window 24, trip <= 0.50,\n     |            order-insensitive) fires on all three pathological runs (calls 88/151/196) and NEVER\n     |            on the clean one.\n     `-- 895c (P1) BLOCKER: plan-review fails CLOSED on a provider error. Two runs minutes apart, very\n                  different plan text, byte-identical failure at `review@then/prerequisite_verify`\n                  (\"Grammar compilation timed out\", Bedrock). One 400 degrades EVERY finding to\n                  `no-verification`; no partial credit. CAVEAT: other reviews passed fine the same day\n                  (e369, 941b, 8390) — it is NOT globally broken; something about c827's review trips\n                  it. Pin that down; do not overclaim.\n\n    aec1 (P1, in progress) usage instrumentation. Mechanism already found (d166, duplicate, linked):\n         `usage_log.record()` NO-OPS unless `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` is set (unset by default), and `_FIELDS`\n         (`usage_log.py:32`) records `requests` but NOT `tool_calls` — the axis separating a loop from\n         breadth AND the second kill path. A default-off recorder is functionally no recorder.\n\n--- DESIGN DECISIONS ALREADY TAKEN (do not relitigate) ---\n* **Flat forced landing: WITHDRAWN.** It truncates a healthy run and produces a worse verdict on a\n  gate that SIGNS. Operator intent: bound failures, not breadth.\n* **Finalizer routing: ADOPTED.** Route detection into the shipped tool-free finalizer\n  (`completion_recovery.py:453-521`), which assembles clean per-criterion evidence, instead of landing\n  on a history that just looped 144 times. Feasibility proven (E5): an exception raised inside the\n  toolset filter predicate propagates cleanly and survives in `__cause__`.\n  **Caveat that would be a NEW bug:** it currently surfaces as `LLMUnavailableError`, because\n  `interpret_failure`'s third branch (`structured_run.py:110-155`) sweeps unrecognised exceptions into\n  the provider-outage bucket. A runaway misreported as a provider outage is the exact defect class\n  being removed — a typed branch must go AHEAD of that fallback.\n* **Both kill paths must be bounded.** `tool_step_limit` gates model REQUESTS; `tool_calls_limit`\n  (= 2x request_limit) is a second independent path enforced on PROJECTED usage\n  (`pydantic_ai/usage.py:412-419`). Measured: at 3 calls/request, limits of 239, 216 AND 180 all still\n  die. A fix setting only `tool_step_limit` passes a 1-call-per-request test and fails in production.\n* **The discriminator predicate fires 6x per run step** — memoise once per `run_step`, not per tool.\n* **AC-split fallback (two runs, full context, half the ACs each): TESTED, NOT SUFFICIENT alone.**\n  Criteria partition cleanly, but both halves still died, and SPLIT-B spent MORE per criterion (23.9)\n  than the full run (17.0) — variance swamps the halving. Viable only WITH the actuator. Two\n  signatures over disjoint criteria subsets materially changes what an attestation MEANS: that needs\n  an operator decision, not a quiet implementation.\n\n--- OPEN DECISIONS FOR THE OPERATOR ---\n1. **f020** — keep or delete the dormant absence-probe port. Deleting removes DC's only import of the\n   shared `classify_probe_response`, weakening e369 AC5's both-backends claim. Blocks epic 5796.\n2. **AC-split attestation semantics**, if that route is wanted.\n3. **How deep to go** on the gate cluster — each defect was found by refusing to force a close, and\n   the chain is now four levels below the original goal.\n\n--- OTHER OPEN WORK ---\n5796 (follow-on epic): 4a6d (a THIRD live-DC channel with the weak readiness gate — it REPORTS rather\nthan fails, so a fast run silently commits a wrong contract), a3fa (capability casts unguarded), f020.\nUnparented by deliberate decision: 6194 / 6a74 / 9a46 (Cloud-surface defects found by DC parity\nguards — real, but not DC feature delivery), a9bd (capability-map trust; operator ordered unparented),\n8a5e (four MORE vacuous AST guards — third occurrence of the class), ed5e (57 tickets still orphaned\nby the 2026-08-03 reconciler parent-clear incident, stuck because their parents are closed).\nc827 siblings: fd84, 8b97, 218e, 4c2b, 354d. Plus 6ec8 (child-closure proof computed then hidden from\nthe verifier), 3787 (P1, Rebase-If-Necessary submits an unvalidated tree), df3a (fix in flight as\nGerrit 1408, another session).\n\n--- OPERATIONAL RULES THAT COST REAL TIME TO LEARN ---\n* **CI selects by MARKER**: `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 4 --dist worksteal -q`.\n  A `tests/unit` path subset MISSES unmarked tests under `tests/integration/` — that gap cost a CI\n  cycle (the `EXPECTED_LEAF_PAIRS` drift guard). ~10,400 tests vs ~8,000.\n* **`git commit --amend -F -` DROPS the `Change-Id`**, minting a new change instead of a patchset —\n  which is how a green-but-WRONG change (1398) was left sitting submittable.\n* **3787 discipline**: if `gerrit/main` moved past your base, rebase LOCALLY and re-gate rather than\n  letting Gerrit rebase server-side into a tree CI never validated. `main` moved ~every 20-30 min.\n* **Store lock contention is real** (ab54/7084): `rebar edit` can fail with `flock: could not acquire\n  lock after 60s` and NOT land. Retry, and confirm every write by READING THE OUTPUT.\n* **Attestations go `stale-code` once their own change lands** — expect a re-review before every\n  close. `--status` is HEAD-relative: run it from the same worktree you will close from.\n* **The GitHub mirror LAGS Gerrit.** `gh workflow run --ref main` can resolve to the PRE-merge commit.\n  Check the resolved `headSha` against the merge commit before letting a 45-minute run proceed.\n* **Agent tools come from the INSTALLED rebar, not the attested snapshot** (`runner.py:341`). After\n  merging a fix under `llm/`, confirm the interpreter you will run from actually has it.\n* **AN EMPTY OR CLEAN SCAN IS NOT EVIDENCE UNTIL THE SCAN ITSELF IS CHECKED.** Eight false conclusions\n  in one session: a regex missing `Jira DC` (space); `rebar list` carrying NO `description` field;\n  grepping a stale working tree instead of `gerrit/main`; `grep -oE` splicing across\n  `executed=62 skipped=1`; `grep '^FAILED'` defeated by ANSI codes; `grep -c` against a path that did\n  not exist; `pgrep` matching its OWN shell wrapper; and a wrong path guess nearly recorded as an AC9\n  violation. Control scans in BOTH directions.\n* **A mutation check only tests the axes its fixture varies.** Change 1398 passed BOTH gates with a\n  real bug because its fixture hardcoded `remote_parent_id: None`.\n\n--- THE FINDINGS THAT OUTLIVE THIS EPIC ---\n1. **Gates passing != correct.** 1398 held `LLM-Review +1` AND `Verified +1` while containing a bug\n   that would have alerted on every pass for every affected ticket. Caught only by adversarial\n   self-review.\n2. **`search_files` was LYING repo-wide** (fixed, Gerrit 1433): it returned `(no matches)` whenever\n   `path` named a FILE. Measured in one run — 48% of calls passed a file path, 100% of those returned\n   \"no matches\", 26 of 29 had the literal genuinely present. A **false-FAIL vector on a SIGNED gate**;\n   past verdicts that turned on a narrowed search are suspect.\n3. **\"Computed then discarded\" is a pattern, not an incident** — the repetition diagnostic built by\n   `interpret_failure` and dropped; the child-closure proof collapsed to a boolean (6ec8); the usage\n   recorder that no-ops by default (aec1/d166). Three instances in one subsystem.\n4. **Vacuous guards are a recurring class** — 59b2, then 34c2, and 8a5e documents four more live. A\n   guard that cannot fail reads as protection while providing none.\n5. **Authored prose gets read as measurement.** 9790's error message asserted a rule (\"no\n   `customfield_*` => plugin still starting => budget too short\") that was wrong AND self-confirming —\n   an empty custom-field inventory is exactly what a PROJECTLESS instance returns. It misdiagnosed its\n   own author, then me, then the ticket I wrote from it. The real cause was an ordering deadlock and\n   the fix LOWERED the budget 600s -> 120s. Same class as two capability-map retractions.\n6. **20 of 205 epics carry a FORCE_CLOSE record.** Large epics have been bypassing this gate for a\n   long time; the largest ever closed THROUGH it has 19 descendants. e369 (59) is the first that\n   refused to go quietly — which is how this entire cluster surfaced."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785949586392891001,"uuid":"47dca266-4f3c-4466-97de-c4f89d7e4d74"}